he other specs, though I'm not certain of the exact number, so I should construct the task description to point the agent toward the right file based on the use case identifier rel01.0-uc001-wc. I need to figure out the test suite location and understand the task structure. The test suites are documented at docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-[release-id].yaml, so there should be a test-rel-01.0.yaml for the wc release. Since I'm proposing a single task focused on the earliest incomplete release (rel01.0 for wc), I need to reconcile the requirement to split implementation and tests into separate tasks with the fact that the test suite spec already exists as a completed documentation deliverable outside the generation scope. So the question becomes whether I'm proposing the implementation task or if there's something else I'm missing about the task structure.\n\nLooking at the actual code status, rel00.0 is complete with existing implementations, while rel01.0 only has the wc use case with no code yet. The next task is implementing the wc command itself, which means I should propose the implementation task for cmd/wc. I'm starting to think through what the wc implementation would need to support—the standard GNU wc flags like byte count and character count. I'm trying to figure out the correct PRD filename for the wc specification—the implementation status mentions it's complete, so there must be a corresponding requirements document in the product-requirements directory, but I'm not certain of the numbering scheme to reference it properly. I'm trying to figure out the PRD numbering scheme—there's a clear gap between prd003 and prd011, and I'm wondering if the missing numbers correspond to utilities like wc, cat, sponge, and du based on the release roadmap. Rather than guessing at the exact numbering, I think the best approach is to reference the PRD by its likely filename in the specs directory and let the system find it. I'm running into a real constraint here—the instructions require anchoring tasks to specific PRD requirement IDs, but I don't have access to the actual PRD content and can't read files to find them. The project context doesn't include the PRD details either. I think the best approach is to reference the PRD as required reading and describe the wc implementation requirements based on what I can infer from the project structure, even though I can't cite exact requirement IDs. I'm realizing the pragmatic approach is to reference the PRD in required_reading and write generic requirements that map to its structure, letting the stitch agent handle the actual PRD content. For the task title, I'll keep it simple like \"cmd/wc implementation\" without trying to guess specific requirement IDs I don't have access to.\n\nLooking at the test suite structure, wc is in rel01.0, so I need to propose the implementation task first—creating the main.go file with line, word, and byte counting functionality—before the tests, since they should be split per the guidelines.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what the wc implementation actually needs to cover: the various flags like -l, -w, -c, -m, and -L for different count types, handling multiple files with totals, reading from stdin, and proper exit codes. The implementation should be around 150-200 lines.\n\nFor the task description, I'll reference the PRD using a pattern like the other specs, though I'm not entirely certain of the exact numbering scheme for the utility PRDs—the magefiles uses prd011, which suggests the utilities might follow a different range than the initial 001-003 sequence. I'm settling on the wc command implementation as the task, and I need to specify the required reading materials—the wc PRD, use case spec, test suite, and existing code patterns to follow—along with creating the main.go file for the cmd/wc package. For requirements, I'll focus on the core functionality and test structure. I see the pattern now from the existing source files—they use specific PRD identifiers like \"prd003-format R2.4\" and \"prd002-sys R1.1, R1.2\". So for the wc implementation, I should follow that same convention and anchor the task to the wc PRD requirements, letting the stitch agent pull the specific requirement IDs from the PRD file I reference in required_reading. I'm mapping out what's already been completed across the releases—the first four use cases are done with their respective packages and commands in place. The next priority is building the `wc` command for rel01.0, followed by `cat`, `sponge`, and `du` in subsequent releases.\n\nFor the `wc` implementation task, I need to keep it focused on core requirements without inventing design decisions I haven't derived from the PRD. I'll write a task that references the actual PRD file so the stitch agent can pull the specific requirement IDs and details, rather than guessing at what the implementation should include. I need to implement the wc command following the established pattern where main() delegates to run() for the actual logic and exit code handling. The implementation needs to support all counting modes—lines, words, bytes, characters, and maximum line length—each with their own flags, handle multiple files with totals, read from stdin when no files are provided, and return the appropriate exit codes based on file read success or failure. I don't have the actual PRD file numbers, so I need to give the stitch agent a clear way to locate it—I'll point them to the specs directory with a glob pattern for any wc-related PRD. Since I'm mapping requirements without the actual PRD in front of me, I'm essentially writing meta-requirements that instruct the agent to follow whatever the PRD specifies, which is the best approach given what I have to work with.\n\nNow I'm looking at the existing code patterns—the version command shows a simple structure with a version variable, main calling run with arguments, and basic flag handling. For wc, I'd need something similar but with more complex flag parsing to handle line, word, character, and byte counts. I think I have enough context to write solid task requirements focused on the key functional areas: counting operations, flag parsing, input handling, and output formatting. I need to decide whether to implement first or write tests first, and the guidelines are clear that implementation comes before tests when they're split into separate tasks. Since I'm creating just the implementation task, I should focus on that and reference the necessary documentation like the wc PRD, use cases, and existing command structure examples. captureOutput pattern)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/wc/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"wc utility: flag parsing, counting logic (lines/words/bytes/chars/max-line-length), multi-file handling, stdin fallback, exit codes\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement the wc command entry point following the cmd/ package pattern: main() calls os.Exit(run(os.Args[1:])), run() parses flags and returns an int exit code. Parse all flags specified in the wc PRD.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement counting logic for all modes specified in the wc PRD: lines, words, bytes, characters, and maximum line length. When no flags are given, use the default mode specified in the PRD.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Handle multiple file arguments: count each file independently, print per-file results, and print a totals line when more than one file is given. When no file arguments are given, read from stdin.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement exit code behavior per the wc PRD: exit 0 on success, exit 1 when any named file cannot be opened or read. Print a diagnostic to stderr for each inaccessible file and continue processing remaining files.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow cmd/version/main.go pattern: package main, main() delegates to run() which returns an exit code as int.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use Go standard library only (flag, fmt, bufio, io, os, unicode/utf8). No external dependencies.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Read the wc PRD (found via globbing docs/specs/product-requirements/*wc*) for exact flag names, output column ordering, field widths, and formatting rules. Implement exactly what the PRD specifies.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/wc/main.go exists and compiles with no errors: go build ./cmd/wc/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"All counting modes and flags from the wc PRD are implemented.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Running the compiled binary with no arguments reads stdin and prints default counts to stdout.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Running with multiple file arguments prints per-file lines followed by a total line.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Running with a nonexistent file prints a diagnostic to stderr and exits with code 1.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"Output formatting matches the wc PRD exactly: column widths, field ordering, spacing, and alignment.\"\n\nI need to look up the wc PRD to understand the exact specifications for flags, output format, and behavior before implementing. I'm noticing a conflict between P9's guidance on requirements and the hard constraint limiting tasks to 4 requirements max. The constraint takes precedence, so 4 requirements with 5 acceptance criteria works fine. For design decisions, I only have 2 solid ones derived from the architecture—the cmd/ pattern and standard library only—which falls short of P9's 3-5 range, but I shouldn't invent decisions that aren't grounded in the PRD or architecture. Adding the LC_ALL=C differential approach gives me a third one that's actually justified.\n\nOne more thing I need to clarify: since the source shows both main.go and test files in each cmd/ subdirectory, I should explicitly note in the requirements that this task covers only the implementation file, not tests, so the stitch agent doesn't try to generate test code. I also need to include the copyright header that appears in all existing files. I'm laying out the structure for the wc command implementation with specific requirements for flag parsing, all counting modes from the PRD, and proper file handling with totals and stdin fallback.\n\nThe design follows the existing cmd/version pattern with a main() that delegates to run(), uses only Go's standard library, and includes proper error handling with exit codes and stderr diagnostics for inaccessible files. I'm verifying the specification meets all the requirements—checking that it has the right deliverable type, reading materials, file count, and acceptance criteria. Everything looks aligned with the existing patterns and should fit within the line budget for a wc implementation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":52615,"cache_read_input_tokens":14011,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":52615},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55ab7041-764e-4ac8-866b-9ed82819dcaa","uuid":"bcedd348-5a13-4046-913c-800b9843e9b1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011fZ7MCyqSxEsX5owY4VzvY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge on edge cases. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (one package per utility), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall wrappers), `pkg/format/` (shared output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, excluded from generation).\n\n**Implementation status (from source_code, which overrides prose):**\n- **Done:** `pkg/testutils/` (difftest.go + tests), `pkg/sys/` (fileinfo, terminal + tests), `pkg/format/` (color, column, size + tests), `cmd/version/` (main + tests)\n- rel00.0 is effectively complete — all 4 use cases have code in source_code.\n\n**Current release:** rel01.0 (wc). No `cmd/wc/` code exists. This is the earliest incomplete release.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nrel00.0 is done (source_code confirms pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/version all exist). The next release is **rel01.0 — wc**. Per P2, implementation and tests must be split. The implementation task comes first (P8). One task is the maximum allowed by constraints.\n\n## Proposed Tasks\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/wc core implementation\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (glob for *wc* to find the wc PRD -- read it fully before writing any code)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.0-uc001-wc.yaml (wc use case with end-to-end execution path)\n      - cmd/version/main.go (reference for cmd/ package pattern: main calls run, run returns exit code)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/wc/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"wc utility: flag parsing, line/word/byte/char/max-line-length counting, multi-file with totals, stdin fallback, exit codes\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement the wc command entry point: package main with main() calling os.Exit(run(os.Args[1:])). run() parses all flags specified in the wc PRD and returns an int exit code.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement counting logic for every mode in the wc PRD (lines, words, bytes, characters, max line length). When no mode flags are given, use the default mode the PRD specifies.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Handle file arguments per the wc PRD: count each file independently, print per-file results, print a totals line when more than one file is processed, and read from stdin when no file arguments are given.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement exit codes and error handling per the wc PRD: exit 0 on success, exit 1 when any named file cannot be opened or read, print a diagnostic to stderr for each inaccessible file, and continue processing remaining files.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the cmd/version/main.go pattern: package main, main() delegates to run(args []string) int, os.Exit called only in main().\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use Go standard library only (flag, fmt, bufio, io, os, unicode/utf8). Do not import external packages.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Include the project copyright header on every file: // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved. and // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/wc/main.go exists and compiles: go build ./cmd/wc/ succeeds with no errors.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"All counting modes and flags specified in the wc PRD are implemented in cmd/wc/main.go.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"echo 'hello world' | go run ./cmd/wc/ prints line, word, and byte counts to stdout and exits 0.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/wc/ /nonexistent prints a diagnostic to stderr and exits with code 1.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/wc/ reports no issues.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":52615,"cache_read_input_tokens":14011,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":52615},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55ab7041-764e-4ac8-866b-9ed82819dcaa","uuid":"f903dbd1-3798-4fb4-a356-84f277069fa2"}
